Both look like this on paper:

```java
interface Behavior { void execute(Context ctx); }
class ConcreteA implements Behavior { public void execute(Context ctx) { /* ... */ } }
class Context {
    private Behavior behavior;
    void setBehavior(Behavior b) { this.behavior = b; }
}
```

As **Strategy**, `Behavior` is `SortStrategy` and a client picks `QuickSort` or `MergeSort` once, based on data size. As [State](state-pattern), `Behavior` is `OrderState` and `PendingState`/`ShippedState` swap themselves out as the order's lifecycle progresses — the class diagram is the same skeleton wearing two different intents.

The tell is in who calls `setBehavior`/`setState`. In real State code, the *current state object itself* triggers the transition:

```java
interface OrderState {
    void next(Order order);
}
class PendingState implements OrderState {
    public void next(Order order) {
        order.setState(new ShippedState());
    }
}
```

`Order.next()` just delegates to `currentState.next(this)` — the state decides what comes after it. Compare this to Strategy, where `Context` never calls `setBehavior` on itself; only external code does.
